1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:03,730
Two timbers meet end to end.

2
00:00:04,200 --> 00:00:06,830
The male piece drops in from above:

3
00:00:07,360 --> 00:00:10,980
a head on a narrow neck slides down into a

4
00:00:11,020 --> 00:00:15,210
socket, and the timber
comes to rest on a seat.

5
00:00:15,533 --> 00:00:18,323
This is the koshikake kama-tsugi.

6
00:00:18,753 --> 00:00:22,363
In English, it's often
called a gooseneck joint.

7
00:00:27,867 --> 00:00:35,317
Two dictionaries read it kama-tsugi, and
they also list kama alone as a short form.

8
00:00:36,227 --> 00:00:39,177
On its own, kama means a sickle.

9
00:00:40,247 --> 00:00:43,897
Two sources compare the
tenon to a kamakubi:

10
00:00:44,347 --> 00:00:48,417
a snake raising its head on
a neck curved like a sickle.

11
00:00:48,667 --> 00:00:52,717
Gooseneck is an English
name, not a translation.

12
00:00:53,347 --> 00:00:56,417
The Japanese name says
nothing about geese.

13
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:03,800
Like the dovetail splice in
episode five, there's a male

14
00:01:03,840 --> 00:01:07,890
piece, the upper one, and
a female, the lower one.

15
00:01:08,400 --> 00:01:12,850
The head and its socket take
about the upper half of the depth.

16
00:01:13,300 --> 00:01:16,290
The lower half is the seat, the koshikake.

17
00:01:16,700 --> 00:01:20,810
A narrow neck carries a
wider head at its end.

18
00:01:21,320 --> 00:01:25,190
On screen are the names a
glossary gives each part.

19
00:01:25,567 --> 00:01:28,377
It goes together like the dovetail.

20
00:01:28,867 --> 00:01:32,817
The male drops straight
down and rests on the seat.

21
00:01:33,607 --> 00:01:39,137
Pull along the timbers, and the flared
back of the head bears against the socket.

22
00:01:39,627 --> 00:01:42,997
In our model, it can't
slide sideways either.

23
00:01:43,907 --> 00:01:46,137
Nothing stops it lifting, though.

24
00:01:50,233 --> 00:01:54,733
The gooseneck's proportions
on screen come from one test

25
00:01:54,773 --> 00:01:58,503
series on hundred-and-twenty
millimeter timbers.

26
00:01:59,233 --> 00:02:03,703
No carpentry source we
found gives standard sizes.

27
00:02:04,133 --> 00:02:09,043
In these drawings, the gooseneck
reaches much further than our dovetail:

28
00:02:09,473 --> 00:02:11,823
a long neck, then a head.

29
00:02:12,733 --> 00:02:16,933
In one national test database,
the gooseneck specimens held

30
00:02:16,973 --> 00:02:21,383
roughly two to three times the
pull of the dovetail specimens.

31
00:02:22,093 --> 00:02:25,033
The timber grades and
joint sizes differed, so

32
00:02:25,073 --> 00:02:28,023
take that as a rough guide, not a rule.

33
00:02:28,400 --> 00:02:33,110
And a longer gooseneck
isn't simply stronger.

34
00:02:34,300 --> 00:02:39,740
Researchers found stress gathers near
the neck, and one study found the

35
00:02:39,780 --> 00:02:44,310
depth of the timbers mattered more
than the length of the splice.

36
00:02:44,733 --> 00:02:48,143
It often ends in a
sudden, brittle failure:

37
00:02:48,593 --> 00:02:51,143
the head or neck shears off.

38
00:02:51,993 --> 00:02:55,923
Sometimes the flared edges
crush into the socket first.

39
00:02:56,873 --> 00:03:01,743
The test database notes it's
often treated as a pin in design:

40
00:03:02,133 --> 00:03:06,683
it holds against a pull, but
resists bending very little.

41
00:03:10,833 --> 00:03:16,033
The Flat 35 house specification
offers it, alongside the

42
00:03:16,073 --> 00:03:20,883
dovetail splice, for sills,
eaves plates and purlins.

43
00:03:21,773 --> 00:03:25,733
For the beams in the outer
walls at second-floor level, it

44
00:03:25,773 --> 00:03:30,823
offers the gooseneck or another
splice, but not the dovetail.

45
00:03:31,167 --> 00:03:35,527
For roof beams, the spec allows
it only where the beam is

46
00:03:35,567 --> 00:03:39,027
about a hundred and twenty
millimeters deep, and then

47
00:03:39,067 --> 00:03:43,217
adds steel straps on both
faces, bolted through.

48
00:03:43,907 --> 00:03:47,577
One option for second-floor
floor beams does the same.

49
00:03:48,207 --> 00:03:50,137
On purlins, two nails.

50
00:03:50,567 --> 00:03:55,607
On a sill, as in episode five,
the public-works specification

51
00:03:55,647 --> 00:04:00,417
puts the anchor bolt in the
upper piece, beside the splice.

52
00:04:04,700 --> 00:04:09,330
A short stub tenon is
sometimes added under the seat.

53
00:04:09,880 --> 00:04:12,170
Its job is to resist twisting.

54
00:04:12,760 --> 00:04:16,790
In one test series, it
added no pulling strength.

55
00:04:17,200 --> 00:04:21,290
And knots or grain run-out
at the splice weaken it.

56
00:04:21,660 --> 00:04:27,190
The test database advises a generous
safety margin, or added hardware.

57
00:04:30,833 --> 00:04:31,833
Next time:

58
00:04:31,993 --> 00:04:34,643
marking out and cutting the gooseneck.

59
00:04:35,533 --> 00:04:37,803
The koshikake kama-tsugi:

60
00:04:38,473 --> 00:04:42,063
a head on a neck, dropped onto a seat.

61
00:04:42,993 --> 00:04:48,863
It holds against a pull, but
engineers often model it as a pin.
